A Skribisto project is a single `.skrib` file (or, if you chose the bundle format when you
created it, a folder) holding everything you write: the manuscript's structure, every scene's
text and synopsis, and the project's own settings. You open one from the Launcher, and the
Launcher is also where you start a new one.

## The Launcher

The Launcher's sidebar has four sections: *Works*, *Examples*, *Learn*, and *About*. *Works* is
where the first ten minutes happen.

At the top of *Works* sits a *Search works* field, an *Open* button, a *From documents…* button,
and *New Work*. *Open* shows a native file picker for an existing `.skrib` file. *From
documents…* starts a project from a folder of files you already have (see
[importing documents](:help-import-documents)). *New Work* opens the New Work dialog: a name,
an optional author, whether to save as a single file or a bundle, where to put it, a default
language, and a template, *None*, *Empty Novel*, *Light Novel*, *Novel*, or *Notebook*, each
laying down a different starting structure. *Create Work* opens the result straight away.

Below that is *Recent Works*: every project you have opened before, filtered live as you type
in the search field. The list is empty the first time you run Skribisto; open or create your
first project from here.

*Examples* lists a few bundled sample projects. Opening one hands you a private working copy,
so you can look around, edit anything, and never damage the original.

## What a project contains

A project's writing lives in its *Binder*: an ordered, indentable list of items, such as parts,
chapters, scenes, and notes. Nothing about that order is fixed; you can add, rename, indent, and
rearrange items as the book takes shape. What each item is allowed to be, and what it can hold,
follows a small set of rules; this page is only about finding your way around.

## The Binder panel and opening an item

The Binder panel, on the leading side of the window, shows that list as a tree. Press `F9`, or
use *View ▸ Outline*, to show or hide it. Clicking an item (or pressing Enter on a highlighted
one) opens it in the editor.

Every writing item, a scene or a note, shows two things together: its main text and its
*Synopsis*, a short summary you keep beside it. Both are visible by default, and both are yours
to fill in whenever you like; an empty synopsis never blocks you from writing the scene itself,
and the other way around.

## Adding things

The Binder panel's header carries a *＋* button next to the item type it currently recommends,
for example "＋ Scene" when a scene is selected. Its dropdown lists everything else you could add
in that spot, chapters, notes, folders, and the rest, each placed where it makes sense given what
you have selected. There is no single "New Item"; what you can create, and where it lands,
depends on where you are in the tree.

That is enough to start writing. For how the pieces of a book fit together, from chapters and
scenes down to notes and paratext, see [the writing model](:help-writing-model).
